Reason: port: with frame_rate = 60 the bathtub Bullet Bills (TBathtubKiller) blink and smoke at their 30 fps rate; the nose blink and the smoke interval step on the calc-anim pass, which comes every frame, so the nose blinked twice as fast and they smoked twice as often; the puff's keep-alive emitter is requested for the same 1/30 s as at 30
--- a/src/Enemy/BathtubKiller.cpp
+++ b/src/Enemy/BathtubKiller.cpp
@@ -396,6 +396,32 @@ void TBathtubKiller::bind()
 	mLinearVelocity = nextPos - mPosition;
 }
 
+#ifdef TARGET_PC
+#include <System/MarDirector.hpp>
+
+// Port: the Bullet Bill's calc-anim pass blinks its nose (unk1FC steps
+// unk1F8 between 0 and 1) and counts unk1D4 up to mSLSmokeInterval between
+// smoke puffs. With frame_rate = 60 that pass comes every frame, twice as
+// often per second as at 30, so the nose blinked twice as fast and it
+// smoked twice as often. The blink takes half a step at 60 (the same per
+// second, and smooth); the smoke is counted once per 1/30 s: on the calc
+// pass of every other frame at 60 (the director counts one unk5C per tick,
+// two a frame). The puff is a keep-alive request that TMarioParticleManager
+// kills on the first pass it is not repeated, so at 30 it lives one frame,
+// 1/30 s; on the frame after a puff (unk1D4 == -1, set only at 60) request
+// it again so it lives as long.
+extern "C" int port_fps60_active;
+static inline bool BathtubKillerPortCalcStep()
+{
+	return !port_fps60_active || (gpMarDirector->unk5C & 2) != 0;
+}
+
+static inline f32 BathtubKillerPortStep(f32 step)
+{
+	return port_fps60_active ? 0.5f * step : step;
+}
+#endif
+
